 "w81", "text": "design"}, {"id": "w82", "text": "problems,"}, {"id": "w83", "text": "although"}, {"id": "w84", "text": "they"}, {"id": "w85", "text": "cannot"}, {"id": "w86", "text": "establish"}, {"id": "w87", "text": "how"}, {"id": "w88", "text": "common"}, {"id": "w89", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w90", "text": "fault"}, {"id": "w91", "text": "is"}, {"id": "w92", "text": "across"}, {"id": "w93", "text": "all"}, {"id": "w94", "text": "products"}, {"id": "w95", "text": "sold."}, {"id": "w96", "text": "A"}, {"id": "w97", "text": "cafe"}, {"id": "w98", "text": "sees"}, {"id": "w99", "text": "only"}, {"id": "w100", "text": "the"}, {"id": "w101", "text": "objects"}, {"id": "w102", "text": "that"}, {"id": "w103", "text": "people"}, {"id": "w104", "text": "choose"}, {"id": "w105", "text": "to"}, {"id": "w106", "text": "bring."}, {"id": "w107", "text": "Manufacturers"}, {"id": "w108", "text": "could"}, {"id": "w109", "text": "still"}, {"id": "w110", "text": "use"}, {"id": "w111", "text": "this"}, {"id": "w112", "text": "limited"}, {"id": "w113", "text": "evidence"}, {"id": "w114", "text": "to"}, {"id": "w115", "text": "identify"}, {"id": "w116", "text": "components"}, {"id": "w117", "text": "worth"}, {"id": "w118", "text": "investigating."}, {"id": "w119", "text": "The"}, {"id": "w120", "text": "strongest"}, {"id": "w121", "text": "argument"}, {"id": "w122", "text": "for"}, {"id": "w123", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w124", "text": "cafe"}, {"id": "w125", "text": "is"}, {"id": "w126", "text": "consequently"}, {"id": "w127", "text": "educational"}, {"id": "w128", "text": "rather"}, {"id": "w129", "text": "than"}, {"id": "w130", "text": "statistical:"}, {"id": "w131", "text": "a"}, {"id": "w132", "text": "successful"}, {"id": "w133", "text": "visit"}, {"id": "w134", "text": "can"}, {"id": "w135", "text": "change"}, {"id": "w136", "text": "the"}, {"id": "w137", "text": "way"}, {"id": "w138", "text": "someone"}, {"id": "w139", "text": "thinks"}, {"id": "w140", "text": "about"}, {"id": "w141", "text": "ownership,"}, {"id": "w142", "text": "even"}, {"id": "w143", "text": "when"}, {"id": "w144", "text": "the"}, {"id": "w145", "text": "object"}, {"id": "w146", "text": "itself"}, {"id": "w147", "text": "remains"}, {"id": "w148", "text": "broken."}]'::jsonb, '["w14", "w57", "w80"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A repair cafe brings volunteers and residents together to fix household objects. Its purpose is not simply to save the price of a replacement. By explaining each step, volunteers help owners understand why an object failed and how to maintain it. Some visitors arrive expecting a free repair service, however, and feel disappointed when they must participate. Organisers therefore describe the learning element clearly before accepting bookings. They also record items that cannot be repaired. These records can reveal recurring design problems, although they cannot establish how common a fault is across all products sold. A cafe sees only the objects that people choose to bring. Manufacturers could still use this limited evidence to identify components worth investigating. The strongest argument for a cafe is consequently educational rather than statistical: a successful visit can change the way someone thinks about ownership, even when the object itself remains broken.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-031', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Repair cafes', 'The final word is omitted from this practice recording. Select the word that completes the speaker''s meaning.', 120, 0, 0, 1, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'The conclusion ends with understanding. This practice uses a pause, not a recorded beep.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "understanding"}, {"id": "B", "text": "permission"}, {"id": "C", "text": "competition"}, {"id": "D", "text": "decoration"}]'::jsonb, '["A"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A repair cafe brings volunteers and residents together to fix household objects. Its purpose is not simply to save the price of a replacement. By explaining each step, volunteers help owners understand why an object failed and how to maintain it. Some visitors arrive expecting a free repair service, however, and feel disappointed when they must participate. Organisers therefore describe the learning element clearly before accepting bookings. They also record items that cannot be repaired. These records can reveal recurring design problems, although they cannot establish how common a fault is across all products sold. A cafe sees only the objects that people choose to bring. Manufacturers could still use this limited evidence to identify components worth investigating. The strongest argument for a cafe is consequently educational rather than statistical: a successful visit can change the way someone thinks about ownership, even when the object itself remains broken. The object may remain broken, but the visitor can still leave with greater ...', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-032', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'write-from-dictation', 'Write from Dictation', 'Repair cafes', 'Listen to the sentence and type exactly what you hear.', 60, 0, 0, 9, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Reference sentence: The workshop accepts bookings through the community centre website.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '["The workshop accepts bookings through the community centre website."]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The workshop accepts bookings through the community centre website.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-033', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'summarize-spoken-text', 'Summarize Spoken Text', 'Repair cafes', 'Listen to the talk and summarise its main points in 50-70 words. You have 10 minutes. Practice feedback is an approximate content/form check, not an official PTE score.', 600, 0, 0, 10, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Repair cafes teach owners maintenance skills through participation, while records of failed repairs can suggest design issues but cannot establish their prevalence because visitors bring a self-selected sample of products.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '["repair", "owners", "learning", "records", "sample"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A repair cafe brings volunteers and residents together to fix household objects. Its purpose is not simply to save the price of a replacement. By explaining each step, volunteers help owners understand why an object failed and how to maintain it. Some visitors arrive expecting a free repair service, however, and feel disappointed when they must participate. Organisers therefore describe the learning element clearly before accepting bookings. They also record items that cannot be repaired. These records can reveal recurring design problems, although they cannot establish how common a fault is across all products sold. A cafe sees only the objects that people choose to bring. Manufacturers could still use this limited evidence to identify components worth investigating. The strongest argument for a cafe is consequently educational rather than statistical: a successful visit can change the way someone thinks about ownership, even when the object itself remains broken. Consider a visitor with a desk lamp. A volunteer may find that a replacement switch is no longer available. The visit has not restored the lamp, but the owner has learned why access to spare parts matters. That knowledge may influence a later purchase. Another visitor may decide to clean an appliance regularly after seeing how dust affected its performance. Neither outcome appears in a count of completed repairs. Organisers should therefore ask what participants learned, as well as whether their objects function again. Enthusiasm should not replace safety: volunteers need clear limits on their work and should refer unsuitable jobs to qualified professionals.', 'Repair cafes teach owners maintenance skills through participation, while records of failed repairs can suggest design issues but cannot establish their prevalence because visitors bring a self-selected sample of products. The speaker illustrates why a simple measure can be misleading and argues that decisions should reflect the purpose of the activity as well as the limitations of the available evidence.', NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-034', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcma', 'MCQ Multiple Answer', 'Night-time bus trials', 'Listen and select all supported statements.', 150, 0, 0, 2, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Although a late bus trial attracted few passengers, interviews revealed employment benefits and limited publicity, leading researchers to recommend broader evaluation and comparison with alternatives before a final funding decision.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "The trial ran on every route in the city."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Advertising had missed some potential passengers."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Shared taxis were rejected without comparison."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Some workers used the service to travel home after evening shifts."}]'::jsonb, '["B", "D"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A city transport team tested a later bus service on two routes for six weeks. Passenger numbers were modest, so an initial report recommended cancellation. Interviews subsequently showed that several shift workers had accepted evening work because the buses gave them a reliable way home. Counting journeys alone had overlooked this benefit. The team also discovered that publicity had reached regular passengers but few potential new users. A longer trial with wider advertising might therefore produce different results. Nevertheless, the interviews did not prove that demand would eventually cover operating costs. The researchers recommended comparing the service with alternatives, including a shared taxi scheme. They proposed recording who benefited, how reliably vehicles arrived and the cost per completed journey. The council postponed its final decision until this broader evidence was available.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-035', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Night-time bus trials', 'What is the main point of the recording?', 150, 0, 0, 1, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Although a late bus trial attracted few passengers, interviews revealed employment benefits and limited publicity, leading researchers to recommend broader evaluation and comparison with alternatives before a final funding decision.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Interviews established that ticket sales would cover all costs."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The council permanently cancelled both routes after six weeks."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Passenger volume is irrelevant to transport planning."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A service should be evaluated against several clearly stated objectives."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A city transport team tested a later bus service on two routes for six weeks. Passenger numbers were modest, so an initial report recommended cancellation. Interviews subsequently showed that several shift workers had accepted evening work because the buses gave them a reliable way home. Counting journeys alone had overlooked this benefit. The team also discovered that publicity had reached regular passengers but few potential new users. A longer trial with wider advertising might therefore produce different results. Nevertheless, the interviews did not prove that demand would eventually cover operating costs. The researchers recommended comparing the service with alternatives, including a shared taxi scheme. They proposed recording who benefited, how reliably vehicles arrived and the cost per completed journey. The council postponed its final decision until this broader evidence was available.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-036', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-highlight-correct-summary', 'Highlight Correct Summary', 'Night-time bus trials', 'Listen and select the best summary.', 150, 0, 0, 1, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Although a late bus trial attracted few passengers, interviews revealed employment benefits and limited publicity, leading researchers to recommend broader evaluation and comparison with alternatives before a final funding decision.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Interviews established that ticket sales would cover all costs."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The council permanently cancelled both routes after six weeks."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Passenger volume is irrelevant to transport planning."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Although a late bus trial attracted few passengers, interviews revealed employment benefits and limited publicity, leading researchers to recommend broader evaluation and comparison with alternatives before a final funding decision."}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A city transport team tested a later bus service on two routes for six weeks. Passenger numbers were modest, so an initial report recommended cancellation. Interviews subsequently showed that several shift workers had accepted evening work because the buses gave them a reliable way home. Counting journeys alone had overlooked this benefit. The team also discovered that publicity had reached regular passengers but few potential new users. A longer trial with wider advertising might therefore produce different results. Nevertheless, the interviews did not prove that demand would eventually cover operating costs. The researchers recommended comparing the service with alternatives, including a shared taxi scheme. They proposed recording who benefited, how reliably vehicles arrived and the cost per completed journey. The council postponed its final decision until this broader evidence was available.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
