way home. Counting journeys alone had overlooked this benefit. The team also discovered that publicity had reached regular passengers but few potential new users. A longer trial with wider advertising might therefore produce different results. Nevertheless, the interviews did not prove that demand would eventually cover operating costs. The researchers recommended comparing the service with alternatives, including a shared taxi scheme. They proposed recording who benefited, how reliably vehicles arrived and the cost per completed journey. The council postponed its final decision until this broader evidence was available.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-039', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Night-time bus trials', 'The final word is omitted from this practice recording. Select the word that completes the speaker''s meaning.', 120, 0, 0, 1, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'The conclusion ends with evaluation. This practice uses a pause, not a recorded beep.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "permission"}, {"id": "B", "text": "competition"}, {"id": "C", "text": "decoration"}, {"id": "D", "text": "evaluation"}]'::jsonb, '["D"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A city transport team tested a later bus service on two routes for six weeks. Passenger numbers were modest, so an initial report recommended cancellation. Interviews subsequently showed that several shift workers had accepted evening work because the buses gave them a reliable way home. Counting journeys alone had overlooked this benefit. The team also discovered that publicity had reached regular passengers but few potential new users. A longer trial with wider advertising might therefore produce different results. Nevertheless, the interviews did not prove that demand would eventually cover operating costs. The researchers recommended comparing the service with alternatives, including a shared taxi scheme. They proposed recording who benefited, how reliably vehicles arrived and the cost per completed journey. The council postponed its final decision until this broader evidence was available. Before choosing how to spend public money, the council needs a broader ...', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-040', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'write-from-dictation', 'Write from Dictation', 'Night-time bus trials', 'Listen to the sentence and type exactly what you hear.', 60, 0, 0, 9, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Reference sentence: The last bus leaves the main entrance at midnight.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '["The last bus leaves the main entrance at midnight."]'::jsonb, NULL, 'The last bus leaves the main entrance at midnight.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-041', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'summarize-spoken-text', 'Summarize Spoken Text', 'Night-time bus trials', 'Listen to the talk and summarise its main points in 50-70 words. You have 10 minutes. Practice feedback is an approximate content/form check, not an official PTE score.', 600, 0, 0, 10, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Although a late bus trial attracted few passengers, interviews revealed employment benefits and limited publicity, leading researchers to recommend broader evaluation and comparison with alternatives before a final funding decision.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '["bus", "workers", "publicity", "cost", "evaluation"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A city transport team tested a later bus service on two routes for six weeks. Passenger numbers were modest, so an initial report recommended cancellation. Interviews subsequently showed that several shift workers had accepted evening work because the buses gave them a reliable way home. Counting journeys alone had overlooked this benefit. The team also discovered that publicity had reached regular passengers but few potential new users. A longer trial with wider advertising might therefore produce different results. Nevertheless, the interviews did not prove that demand would eventually cover operating costs. The researchers recommended comparing the service with alternatives, including a shared taxi scheme. They proposed recording who benefited, how reliably vehicles arrived and the cost per completed journey. The council postponed its final decision until this broader evidence was available. Suppose a service carries a nurse who would otherwise be unable to work a late shift. Ticket revenue captures only part of that journey''s value. It would also be misleading to assume that every passenger experiences a benefit of this size. Evaluators need evidence about actual users and their available options. They should distinguish temporary lack of awareness from persistent lack of demand. Weather, shift patterns and the timing of a trial can affect use. A transparent report would present these uncertainties alongside costs, allowing elected representatives to decide which outcomes they value and what support they can provide.', 'Although a late bus trial attracted few passengers, interviews revealed employment benefits and limited publicity, leading researchers to recommend broader evaluation and comparison with alternatives before a final funding decision. The speaker illustrates why a simple measure can be misleading and argues that decisions should reflect the purpose of the activity as well as the limitations of the available evidence.', NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-042', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcma', 'MCQ Multiple Answer', 'Museum object labels', 'Listen and select all supported statements.', 150, 0, 0, 2, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'A museum combined question-based labels with optional detailed information after observing that accessible prompts encouraged family discussion, while recognising that longer visits alone did not demonstrate improved learning.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Detailed information remained available on nearby cards."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Every visitor was required to read the information cards."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Staff asked visitors to explain a feature they had noticed."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The experiment proved that congestion never affected visit length."}]'::jsonb, '["A", "C"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A regional museum replaced several technical labels with questions inviting visitors to examine the objects closely. One label beside a ceramic bowl asked what marks on its base might reveal about production. Visitors spent longer at the display, but the museum did not immediately conclude that they had learned more. Longer visits could indicate curiosity, confusion or simply congestion in a narrow gallery. Staff therefore observed conversations and invited visitors to explain one feature they had noticed. These additional measures suggested that the questions encouraged discussion between adults and children. Some specialists nevertheless preferred the detailed terminology on the original labels. The museum responded by keeping the introductory questions and adding optional information cards nearby. This layered approach offered different levels of detail without requiring everyone to read the same long text.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-043', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Museum object labels', 'What is the main point of the recording?', 150, 0, 0, 1, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'A museum combined question-based labels with optional detailed information after observing that accessible prompts encouraged family discussion, while recognising that longer visits alone did not demonstrate improved learning.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Technical information should be removed from every museum."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Children cannot discuss objects without specialist terminology."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Different layers of information can serve visitors with different needs."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Longer visits always demonstrate more accurate understanding."}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A regional museum replaced several technical labels with questions inviting visitors to examine the objects closely. One label beside a ceramic bowl asked what marks on its base might reveal about production. Visitors spent longer at the display, but the museum did not immediately conclude that they had learned more. Longer visits could indicate curiosity, confusion or simply congestion in a narrow gallery. Staff therefore observed conversations and invited visitors to explain one feature they had noticed. These additional measures suggested that the questions encouraged discussion between adults and children. Some specialists nevertheless preferred the detailed terminology on the original labels. The museum responded by keeping the introductory questions and adding optional information cards nearby. This layered approach offered different levels of detail without requiring everyone to read the same long text.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
