t longer at the display, but the museum did not immediately conclude that they had learned more. Longer visits could indicate curiosity, confusion or simply congestion in a narrow gallery. Staff therefore observed conversations and invited visitors to explain one feature they had noticed. These additional measures suggested that the questions encouraged discussion between adults and children. Some specialists nevertheless preferred the detailed terminology on the original labels. The museum responded by keeping the introductory questions and adding optional information cards nearby. This layered approach offered different levels of detail without requiring everyone to read the same long text.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-047', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-select-missing-word', 'Select Missing Word', 'Museum object labels', 'The final word is omitted from this practice recording. Select the word that completes the speaker''s meaning.', 120, 0, 0, 1, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'The conclusion ends with choice. This practice uses a pause, not a recorded beep.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "competition"}, {"id": "B", "text": "decoration"}, {"id": "C", "text": "choice"}, {"id": "D", "text": "permission"}]'::jsonb, '["C"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A regional museum replaced several technical labels with questions inviting visitors to examine the objects closely. One label beside a ceramic bowl asked what marks on its base might reveal about production. Visitors spent longer at the display, but the museum did not immediately conclude that they had learned more. Longer visits could indicate curiosity, confusion or simply congestion in a narrow gallery. Staff therefore observed conversations and invited visitors to explain one feature they had noticed. These additional measures suggested that the questions encouraged discussion between adults and children. Some specialists nevertheless preferred the detailed terminology on the original labels. The museum responded by keeping the introductory questions and adding optional information cards nearby. This layered approach offered different levels of detail without requiring everyone to read the same long text. Rather than making every label longer, the museum offered visitors a ...', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-048', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'write-from-dictation', 'Write from Dictation', 'Museum object labels', 'Listen to the sentence and type exactly what you hear.', 60, 0, 0, 9, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Reference sentence: Detailed information cards are available beside the exhibition entrance.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '["Detailed information cards are available beside the exhibition entrance."]'::jsonb, NULL, 'Detailed information cards are available beside the exhibition entrance.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-049', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'summarize-spoken-text', 'Summarize Spoken Text', 'Museum object labels', 'Listen to the talk and summarise its main points in 50-70 words. You have 10 minutes. Practice feedback is an approximate content/form check, not an official PTE score.', 600, 0, 0, 10, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'A museum combined question-based labels with optional detailed information after observing that accessible prompts encouraged family discussion, while recognising that longer visits alone did not demonstrate improved learning.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '["museum", "labels", "discussion", "detail", "learning"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'A regional museum replaced several technical labels with questions inviting visitors to examine the objects closely. One label beside a ceramic bowl asked what marks on its base might reveal about production. Visitors spent longer at the display, but the museum did not immediately conclude that they had learned more. Longer visits could indicate curiosity, confusion or simply congestion in a narrow gallery. Staff therefore observed conversations and invited visitors to explain one feature they had noticed. These additional measures suggested that the questions encouraged discussion between adults and children. Some specialists nevertheless preferred the detailed terminology on the original labels. The museum responded by keeping the introductory questions and adding optional information cards nearby. This layered approach offered different levels of detail without requiring everyone to read the same long text. The distinction between attention and understanding matters in many learning environments. A colourful display can hold attention without explaining a difficult idea. Conversely, a brief interaction may help someone notice a feature they would otherwise miss. A question about tool marks invited visitors to look back at the bowl rather than just read a description. Staff could then listen for explanations grounded in observation. This was more informative than recording time alone. The museum also considered readers with different language backgrounds, making the first layer clear while preserving precise terms for those who wanted them.', 'A museum combined question-based labels with optional detailed information after observing that accessible prompts encouraged family discussion, while recognising that longer visits alone did not demonstrate improved learning. The speaker illustrates why a simple measure can be misleading and argues that decisions should reflect the purpose of the activity as well as the limitations of the available evidence.', NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-050', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcma', 'MCQ Multiple Answer', 'Shared research equipment', 'Listen and select all supported statements.', 150, 0, 0, 2, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Departments sharing an imaging instrument found that protected maintenance and coordinated scheduling improved reliability despite slightly lower utilisation, demonstrating that successful sharing involves more than reducing purchase costs.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Technicians recommended eliminating calibration."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Preparation time was recorded separately from measurement time."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Every department purchased an additional machine."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Fewer experiments required repetition after the scheduling change."}]'::jsonb, '["B", "D"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'Several university departments agreed to share an expensive imaging instrument instead of buying separate machines. The arrangement reduced initial expenditure, but scheduling soon became difficult. Experiments needed different amounts of preparation, and a short booking sometimes delayed the following user. A committee initially proposed charging departments for every minute of access. Technicians warned that this might discourage necessary cleaning and calibration between sessions. The committee instead introduced protected maintenance periods and required users to estimate preparation time separately from measurement time. It also appointed a coordinator to resolve conflicts. Utilisation fell slightly because the timetable now included maintenance, yet fewer experiments had to be repeated. The departments concluded that keeping the machine continuously occupied was a poor substitute for producing reliable results. Sharing equipment required investment in coordination as well as agreement over purchase costs.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
('pte-expansion-051', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', ARRAY['PTE'], 'listening', 'pte-listening-mcsa', 'MCQ Single Answer', 'Shared research equipment', 'What is the main point of the recording?', 150, 0, 0, 1, 'medium', ARRAY['PTE','Original practice','Expansion 2026'], 'Departments sharing an imaging instrument found that protected maintenance and coordinated scheduling improved reliability despite slightly lower utilisation, demonstrating that successful sharing involves more than reducing purchase costs.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"id": "A", "text": "Sharing equipment removed the need for technicians."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Coordination and maintenance are essential to successful equipment sharing."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Charging by the minute eliminated all scheduling conflicts."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Continuous occupation guarantees reliable measurements."}]'::jsonb, '["B"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'Several university departments agreed to share an expensive imaging instrument instead of buying separate machines. The arrangement reduced initial expenditure, but scheduling soon became difficult. Experiments needed different amounts of preparation, and a short booking sometimes delayed the following user. A committee initially proposed charging departments for every minute of access. Technicians warned that this might discourage necessary cleaning and calibration between sessions. The committee instead introduced protected maintenance periods and required users to estimate preparation time separately from measurement time. It also appointed a coordinator to resolve conflicts. Utilisation fell slightly because the timetable now included maintenance, yet fewer experiments had to be repeated. The departments concluded that keeping the machine continuously occupied was a poor substitute for producing reliable results. Sharing equipment required investment in coordination as well as agreement over purchase costs.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
